      content: "Fundamental Rights in Part III (Art 12-35) are modelled on the US Bill of Rights and constitute the cornerstone of constitutional democracy. Article 12 defines 'State' broadly (government, Parliament, legislatures, local authorities, other authorities). Article 13 provides that laws inconsistent with FRs are void — both pre-constitutional (Art 13(1)) and post-constitutional (Art 13(2)). Originally 7 rights existed; the Right to Property (Art 31) was removed by the 44th Amendment (1978) and made a legal right under Art 300A. FRs are primarily available against the State, though some (Art 17, 23, 24) also bind private individuals. They are not absolute — each right has specified grounds for 'reasonable restrictions'. During National Emergency, Art 19 is automatically suspended; other FRs (except Art 20 and 21) can be suspended by Presidential Order. Article 32 (Right to Constitutional Remedies) allows direct access to the SC through five writs — Ambedkar called it 'the heart and soul of the Constitution'.",
